@description REAPER MCP — Model Context Protocol interface (immersive/spatial audio)
@version 1.22.0
@author James Livingston
@link https://github.com/inseglet-audio/inseglet
@provides
  [darwin] reaper_mcp.dylib https://github.com/inseglet-audio/inseglet/releases/download/v$version/reaper_mcp.dylib
@changelog
  The true-peak bounds now say what they do NOT cover on the low side, and the grid half of the guarantee is a theorem.

  WHAT CHANGES. Nothing you measure. This release moves no number: the twelve-fixture invariant probe reads
  `truePeakDb` and all four disclosed bounds identical before and after. What changes is what the estimator
  tells you about the scope of those bounds.

  THE LOW SIDE HAS A WORST CASE THE SINE NUMBERS CANNOT SEE. All four bounds are derived for a sine. The
  description already said a broadband transient can exceed the GAIN bound slightly. It said nothing about the
  low side, where a reader at the ceiling would reasonably infer that the 0.14 dB measured on programme
  material is close to the worst. It is not. The interpolation error changes sign across the band, so a signal
  band-limited to the passband can carry energy in phase where the filter loses and out of phase where it gains,
  and the ripple then adds coherently instead of cancelling. Such a signal was constructed and driven through
  this meter: it reads about 0.40 dB low -- past `truePeakSineLowBoundDb`, past the single-extremum worst, and
  past the sum of the grid and loss bounds. The description now says so, names it as FOUND BY SEARCH rather than
  computed from the table, and states that the worst over all such signals is not known to be smaller.
  Programme material measured so far still reads within 0.14 dB.

  THE GRID HALF IS NOW A THEOREM. A classical inequality for band-limited functions (Duffin and Schaeffer's,
  through its cosine corollary) gives f(t0 + d) >= M cos(2 pi W d) for every real signal band-limited to W, so
  the exact value at the nearest evaluation instant is within `truePeakGridBoundDb` of the peak for ANY such
  signal, with the band-edge sine as the extremal case. The header no longer calls that extension "standard
  textbook reasoning"; it is a theorem. The same is emphatically not true of the filter half, which is where
  the 0.40 dB lives.

  WHY DISCLOSE IT. A measurement you rely on at the ceiling should tell you where it stops being trustworthy,
  so you can judge for yourself whether its tolerances meet your standards.
